## Finding

The warning is caused by a **second removal of the USB-device → Type-C-partner sysfs link**, in the Type-C class teardown path. It is not a UCSI protocol error and not a kernfs failure.

The relevant chain is:

```text
ucsi_handle_connector_change()
  -> ucsi_unregister_partner()
     -> typec_unregister_partner()
        -> typec_partner_unlink_device()
           -> sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec")
              -> kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()
```

This tree is commit `028ef9c96e96197026887c0f092424679298aae8`, and its shallow history contains the vulnerable implementation.

### 1. UCSI invokes partner teardown

`drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264-1364`:

```c
if (change & UCSI_CONSTAT_CONNECT_CHANGE) {
	...
	if (UCSI_CONSTAT(con, CONNECTED)) {
		...
	} else {
		ucsi_unregister_partner(con);
	}
}
```

The disconnect path is at approximately `ucsi.c:1325`.

`ucsi_unregister_partner()` is at `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138-1154`:

```c
static void ucsi_unregister_partner(struct ucsi_connector *con)
{
	if (!con->partner)
		return;

	typec_set_mode(con->port, TYPEC_STATE_SAFE);
	...
	ucsi_unregister_altmodes(con, UCSI_RECIPIENT_SOP);
	ucsi_unregister_cable(con);
	typec_unregister_partner(con->partner);
	...
	con->partner = NULL;
}
```

The direct call into the Type-C class is `ucsi.c:1151`.

### 2. Type-C unregister removes the USB links

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174-1196`:

```c
void typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)
{
	struct typec_port *port;

	if (IS_ERR_OR_NULL(partner))
		return;

	port = to_typec_port(partner->dev.parent);

	mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);
	if (port->usb2_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);
		port->usb2_dev = NULL;
	}
	if (port->usb3_dev) {
		typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);
		port->usb3_dev = NULL;
	}

	device_unregister(&partner->dev);
	mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);
}
```

The immediate offending helper is `typec_partner_unlink_device()` at `class.c:906-913`:

```c
static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner,
					struct device *dev)
{
	sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");

	if (partner->deattach)
		partner->deattach(partner, dev);
}
```

The warning names `typec`, so the relevant operation is:

```c
sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "typec");
```

The corresponding link is created at `class.c:888-904`:

```c
ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, "typec");
```

### 3. Why the removal can be invalid

USB teardown separately invokes the Type-C detach callback in `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2363`, before the USB device is deleted at `hub.c:2376`:

```c
typec_deattach(port_dev->connector, &udev->dev);
...
device_del(&udev->dev);
```

The Type-C port installs its connector callback at `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:2752-2753`:

```c
port->con.attach = typec_partner_attach;
port->con.deattach = typec_partner_deattach;
```

`typec_partner_deattach()` is at `class.c:2169-2186` and removes the link during USB detach:

```c
partner = typec_get_partner(port);
if (partner) {
	typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev);
	put_device(&partner->dev);
}
```

It then clears `port->usb2_dev` or `port->usb3_dev`.

The important distinction is that `partner->deattach` in `typec_partner_unlink_device()` is a callback supplied in the `typec_partner_desc`; it is **not** the port’s `con.deattach` callback. UCSI does not populate `desc.deattach`, so this is not recursive execution through `typec_partner_deattach()`.

The actual bug is teardown ordering/ownership: both the USB detach side and the UCSI partner-unregister side can own removal of the same partner/device link, while `typec_partner_unlink_device()` unconditionally calls `sysfs_remove_link()` without verifying that the device kobject still has a live kernfs directory.

### 4. Why kernfs prints “no directory”

`drivers/usb/typec/class.c:909` calls `sysfs_remove_link()`.

`fs/sysfs/symlink.c:143-155` obtains the parent from the kobject:

```c
if (!kobj)
	parent = sysfs_root_kn;
else
	parent = kobj->sd;

kernfs_remove_by_name(parent, name);
```

`kernfs_remove_by_name()` invokes `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()`. In this tree, `fs/kernfs/dir.c:1724-1734` contains:

```c
if (!parent) {
	WARN(1, KERN_WARNING
	     "kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\n",
	     name);
	return -ENOENT;
}
```

Thus:

```text
dev->kobj.sd == NULL
    -> kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(NULL, "typec", NULL)
    -> WARN(... "no directory")
    -> -ENOENT
```

`ENOENT` here does not mean that kernfs failed to find a child named `typec` under a valid parent. It means that the **parent directory itself is gone**. The USB device’s sysfs directory has already been detached, or the link has already been removed as part of the competing teardown path.

## Is the fix in this tree?

No.

The vulnerable code is still present:

- `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906-913` — unconditional link removal.
- `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174-1195` — partner unregister removes links using `usb2_dev`/`usb3_dev`.
- `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:2169-2186` — USB detach also removes the partner/device link.
- `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138-1154` — UCSI calls the same partner-unregister path.

There is no `sysfs_delete_link()` use in `drivers/usb/typec` in this tree and no guard that makes the device-side removal safe after the device kobject has been detached.

## Upstream fix

The upstream fix is the USB Type-C class/sysfs-link teardown series generally titled:

> **USB: typec: Fix double removal of typec symlink**

Its essential changes are:

1. stop treating the same USB/Type-C link as independently removable by both teardown paths;
2. use target-aware link deletion (`sysfs_delete_link()`) where the target kobject may already be disappearing; and
3. separate the Type-C class’s link removal from the partner detach callback so the same link is not removed twice.

The exact upstream commit hash is **not present in this checkout**: the repository is shallow and contains only the single commit shown above, with no upstream remote history or patch files. Therefore I cannot responsibly give a hash from this tree. The source-level comparison is nevertheless conclusive: the fix is absent here, and a kernel carrying the Type-C “double removal of typec symlink” fix series should eliminate this warning.
